==Russian==
{{wikipedia|lang=ru}}
===Etymology===
From {{etyl|sla-pro|ru}} {{recons|zolto|lang=sla-pro}} (Old Church Slavonic {{term|злато|tr=zlato|lang=cu}}), from {{etyl|ine-pro|ru}} {{recons|ghəl|lang=ine-pro}}. Cognate with Ukrainian {{term|золото|tr=zoloto|lang=uk}}, Belarusian {{term|золата|tr=zolata|lang=be}}, Bulgarian {{term|злато|tr=zlato|lang=bg}}, Serbian {{term|злато|tr=zlato|lang=sh|sc=Cyrl}}, Slovene {{term|zlato|lang=sl}}, Czech {{term|zlato|lang=cs}}, Slovak {{term|zlato|lang=sk}}, Polish {{term|złoto|lang=pl}}. The Indo-European root is also the source of Proto-Germanic {{recons|gulþan|lang=gem-pro}} (whence English {{term|gold|lang=en}}).

